Reasoning: invalid_grant on a refresh that is still inside our 90-day TTL points at a server-side policy gate, not the token's own validity. Replay one failing refresh to capture the exact error detail.
<|call:replay_token_request|> {"grant_type": "refresh_token", "refresh_token": "rt_8842c1", "client_id": "mobile-ios"}
System Response: 400 invalid_grant {"error_description": "token is valid and not expired"}  <- the token itself verifies fine in isolation
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the replayed token validates yet the grant is refused, so the rejection comes from a policy above token validity, not a corrupt token. Inspect the IdP's session/lifetime policy.
<|call:get_idp_metadata|> {"fields": ["session_policy", "refresh_token_absolute_lifetime"]}
System Response: refresh_token_absolute_lifetime=2592000s (30d); refresh_token_idle_ttl=90d; absolute value last changed at 03:00 UTC
Recovery reasoning: A 30-day absolute cap now overrides our 90-day idle TTL, matching the exact symptom. Confirm who set it and whether it was intentional before reverting.
<|call:query_idp_logs|> {"event": "policy_change", "since": "03:00Z", "field": "refresh_token_absolute_lifetime"}
System Response: actor=terraform-ci change=absolute_lifetime null->2592000 (default injected by a provider-module upgrade, not a security decision)
Recovery reasoning: It was an unintended provider-upgrade default. Restore the absolute lifetime to match the intended 90-day idle TTL.
<|call:set_token_policy|> {"target": "realm/mobile", "patch": {"refresh_token_absolute_lifetime": 7776000}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: applied; refresh now returns 200; invalid_grant rate back to baseline
Resolution: Root cause was a provider-upgrade default capping refresh absolute lifetime at 30d; restoring the 90d value fixed it while keeping idle-TTL rotation intact.

Reasoning: One token passing on api.acme.io but failing on the new billing gateway with 'audience not allowed' is a classic aud-claim scoping problem. Decode the live token to see what audience it actually carries.
<|call:decode_jwt|> {"token": "eyJ", "claims": ["aud", "iss", "scope"]}
System Response: {"aud": "api.acme.io", "iss": "https://idp.acme.io", "scope": "billing.read billing.write"}  <- token has the right billing scopes, so it looks correct
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the scopes are present so the token looks fine, but aud is the single value api.acme.io while the billing gateway expects api-billing.acme.io. Check what audiences that gateway accepts.
<|call:get_client_config|> {"resource_server": "api-billing", "field": "accepted_audiences"}
System Response: accepted_audiences=["api-billing.acme.io"]; zero overlap with the token's aud
Recovery reasoning: The IdP simply never mints the new audience for this client. Fix at the source by mapping both audiences so tokens are valid at both gateways without widening scope.
<|call:update_client_config|> {"target": "client/web-portal", "patch": {"audience_mappings": ["api.acme.io", "api-billing.acme.io"]}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: applied; new tokens carry aud=["api.acme.io","api-billing.acme.io"]; billing gateway returns 200
Resolution: Root cause was the new billing resource server never added to the client's audience mapping; adding it made tokens acceptable at both gateways with no scope change.

Reasoning: 403 insufficient_scope on a single action while everything else works means the access token lacks a scope that one endpoint now requires. Decode a failing user's token to see its scopes.
<|call:decode_jwt|> {"token": "eyJ", "claims": ["scope", "aud", "exp"]}
System Response: {"scope": "openid profile reports.read", "aud": "reports-svc", "exp": 9999999999}  <- valid, unexpired, and already carries reports.read
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the token is valid and even has reports.read, so it looks healthy, but export likely needs a different scope. Find what scope the /export route enforces now.
<|call:grep_app_logs|> {"service": "reports-svc", "match": "insufficient_scope", "fields": ["required_scope", "route"]}
System Response: route=/export required_scope=reports.export present_scopes=[reports.read]
Recovery reasoning: The 14:20 deploy tightened /export to require reports.export. Verify the client is even allowed to request that scope before changing the auth call.
<|call:compare_scopes|> {"client": "web-portal", "requested": ["openid","profile","reports.read"], "allowed": ["openid","profile","reports.read"]}
System Response: reports.export not in client allowed_scopes -> it would be silently dropped even if requested
Recovery reasoning: The real gap is the client registration, not the auth request. Grant reports.export and add it to default scopes so re-issued tokens carry it.
<|call:update_client_config|> {"target": "client/web-portal", "patch": {"allowed_scopes_add": ["reports.export"], "default_scopes_add": ["reports.export"]}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: applied; re-issued tokens include reports.export; /export returns 200
Resolution: Root cause was the deploy adding a reports.export requirement the client was never permitted to request; granting it restored export without loosening other routes.

Reasoning: Intermittent PKCE failures that correlate with multi-tab usage smell like the code_verifier and code_challenge getting crossed between concurrent auth requests, not a crypto bug. Pull logs for a failing exchange to see the verifier/challenge pairing.
<|call:grep_app_logs|> {"service": "spa-bff", "match": "PKCE verification failed", "fields": ["code_challenge", "sent_verifier", "session_id"]}
System Response: code_challenge=S256(Aaa) sent_verifier=Bbb -> the challenge and the verifier belong to two different auth requests
Recovery reasoning: Not a hashing error: the verifier sent does not correspond to the stored challenge, meaning verifier storage is being shared or overwritten across tabs. Find where the verifier is persisted.
<|call:grep_app_logs|> {"service": "spa-bff", "match": "pkce_store", "fields": ["storage", "key"]}
System Response: storage=localStorage key="pkce_verifier" (single fixed key, no per-request namespacing)
Recovery reasoning: A single fixed localStorage key lets a second tab clobber the first tab's verifier, which is exactly the 8% multi-tab failure rate. Namespace the verifier by the state/auth-request id and move it to per-tab storage.
<|call:patch_validator_config|> {"target": "spa-bff/pkce", "patch": {"storage_key": "pkce_verifier:${state}", "scope": "sessionStorage"}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: deployed; concurrent-tab logins now succeed; PKCE failure rate drops to 0%
Resolution: Root cause was a single global storage key letting concurrent tabs overwrite each other's PKCE verifier; per-state keys in sessionStorage fixed it while keeping S256 PKCE enforced.

Reasoning: A single pod rejecting otherwise-valid tokens with iat/exp errors usually means that node's clock is skewed, not the token. Decode a rejected token's time claims first to anchor the validity window.
<|call:decode_jwt|> {"token": "eyJ", "claims": ["iat", "nbf", "exp"]}
System Response: {"iat": 1719460200, "nbf": 1719460200, "exp": 1719463800}  <- claims are well-formed, a clean 60-minute window, nothing wrong with the token
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the token's time claims are perfectly valid, so the fault must be the validator's clock, not the token. Check the offending pod's NTP offset.
<|call:get_ntp_offset|> {"host": "orders-svc-7c9"}
System Response: offset=+47s drift; chronyd last synced 6h ago; status "Leap: not synchronised"
Recovery reasoning: A +47s clock makes freshly issued tokens look 'used before issued', matching the symptom precisely. Force a resync and add a small validation leeway so normal skew never causes false rejects.
<|call:patch_validator_config|> {"target": "orders-svc/jwt", "patch": {"clock_skew_leeway_s": 60, "force_chrony_resync": true}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: chrony forced resync on 7c9 offset->+0.3s; 60s leeway applied fleet-wide; rejections cleared
Resolution: Root cause was +47s NTP drift on one pod rejecting valid tokens; forced resync plus a 60s leeway preserved exp enforcement while tolerating normal skew.

Reasoning: 'kid not found' fleet-wide right after a key rotation usually means our validator's JWKS cache is stale and lacks the new key. First check what the validator currently has cached.
<|call:fetch_jwks|> {"source": "validator_cache", "fields": ["keys[].kid", "fetched_at", "status"]}
System Response: status=OK; cached kids=["rsa-2026-05"]; fetched_at=08:00Z; cache_ttl=24h  <- the cache reports healthy/green but holds only the OLD key
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the cache looks healthy and 'OK', yet it only has the pre-rotation key. Confirm which kid the failing tokens actually demand.
<|call:decode_jwt|> {"token": "eyJ", "part": "header", "claims": ["kid", "alg"]}
System Response: {"kid": "rsa-2026-06", "alg": "RS256"} -> this kid is absent from the cache
Recovery reasoning: Tokens want rsa-2026-06, which the cache lacks. Before forcing a refresh, verify the IdP actually published it and that the old key overlaps so in-flight tokens won't break.
<|call:fetch_jwks|> {"source": "https://idp.acme.io/.well-known/jwks.json", "fields": ["keys[].kid"]}
System Response: live kids=["rsa-2026-05","rsa-2026-06"] (overlap window active)
Recovery reasoning: The new key is live with the old one still present, so a forced refresh is safe. Refresh the cache and cut its TTL so future rotations don't cause another outage.
<|call:patch_validator_config|> {"target": "edge-auth/jwks", "patch": {"force_refresh": true, "cache_ttl_s": 600}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: cache reloaded with both kids; validations succeed; error rate back to 0
Resolution: Root cause was a 24h JWKS cache still serving the pre-rotation key; a forced refresh plus a 10-minute TTL restored validation and hardens future rotations.

Reasoning: redirect_uri_mismatch fires before token issuance, so the redirect_uri the app sends does not exactly match a registered one. Staging working but prod failing points at the prod client's registration. First capture the exact redirect_uri the app is sending.
<|call:trace_redirect_chain|> {"flow": "authorize", "client": "web-portal-prod", "field": "redirect_uri"}
System Response: sent redirect_uri=https://app.acme.io/oauth/callback (authorize accepts it, then returns 400)  <- the app is sending the correct NEW path, so the app looks fine
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the app sends the new path exactly, so the frontend is not the problem; the registration must still hold the old path. Compare against the prod client's registered URIs.
<|call:get_client_config|> {"client": "web-portal-prod", "field": "redirect_uris"}
System Response: redirect_uris=["https://app.acme.io/auth/callback"]  <- still the OLD path; only the staging client was updated
Recovery reasoning: The release shipped the new path but the prod client registration was never updated. Register the new URI and keep the old one during rollout so cached redirects don't break.
<|call:register_redirect_uri|> {"client": "web-portal-prod", "patch": {"redirect_uris_add": ["https://app.acme.io/oauth/callback"]}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: applied; authorize -> callback -> token now succeeds; mismatch errors stop
Resolution: Root cause was the new callback path registered only on the staging client; adding the exact prod URI fixed logins while keeping exact-match redirect validation.

Reasoning: Tokens landing in a third party's Referer logs means the callback page exposes the token in a URL that the browser then leaks cross-origin. Capture a HAR of the callback to see exactly where the token sits.
<|call:get_browser_har|> {"page": "/oauth/callback", "filter": "access_token", "fields": ["url", "status"]}
System Response: url=https://app.acme.io/oauth/callback#access_token=eyJ; status=200; the page renders and login works  <- looks like a normal, successful login
Recovery reasoning: Trap: login succeeds so it seems healthy, but the token rides in the URL fragment. Fragments are not sent in Referer, so something must be promoting it into a query string. Inspect the outbound requests to the analytics origin.
<|call:get_browser_har|> {"page": "/oauth/callback", "filter": "track.vendor.com", "fields": ["request_headers.Referer"]}
System Response: Referer: https://app.acme.io/oauth/callback?access_token=eyJ -> the token is in the query string here, not the fragment
Recovery reasoning: The SPA router rewrites the fragment token into a query param, and the analytics script then leaks it via Referer. Confirm no referrer policy is mitigating it.
<|call:check_referrer_policy|> {"page": "/oauth/callback"}
System Response: Referrer-Policy: not set (browser default leaks the full same-scheme cross-origin Referer)
Recovery reasoning: Two fixes are needed: stop putting tokens in the URL at all, and lock the referrer policy. Move to auth-code + PKCE with form_post and set no-referrer.
<|call:patch_validator_config|> {"target": "spa-bff/auth", "patch": {"response_mode": "form_post", "exchange": "auth_code_pkce", "referrer_policy": "no-referrer"}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: tokens no longer appear in any URL; Referrer-Policy: no-referrer; vendor Referer logs come back clean
Resolution: Root cause was the SPA promoting a fragment token into a query string that leaked via Referer to analytics; auth-code + PKCE form_post plus a no-referrer policy stopped the leak with no loss of analytics.

Reasoning: 'issuer not trusted' for new tokens after an IdP hostname migration means the validator's allowed-issuer list still pins the old issuer. Decode a failing token to confirm its iss.
<|call:decode_jwt|> {"token": "eyJ", "claims": ["iss", "aud"]}
System Response: {"iss": "https://id.acme.io", "aud": "api.acme.io"}  <- a perfectly valid token carrying the new issuer
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the token is valid and correctly carries the new issuer, so the token is not the problem; the validator must still expect the old issuer. Check the gateway's trusted issuer list.
<|call:get_validator_config|> {"resource_server": "api-gateway", "field": "trusted_issuers"}
System Response: trusted_issuers=["https://login.acme.io"]  <- only the OLD issuer is trusted
Recovery reasoning: Before adding the new issuer I must confirm its discovery doc and jwks_uri resolve, otherwise I'd trust an issuer I cannot actually verify.
<|call:get_idp_metadata|> {"issuer": "https://id.acme.io", "fields": ["issuer", "jwks_uri"]}
System Response: issuer=https://id.acme.io jwks_uri=https://id.acme.io/jwks.json (reachable, keys present)
Recovery reasoning: The new issuer is verifiable, so add it alongside the old one for the overlap window rather than replacing it.
<|call:patch_validator_config|> {"target": "api-gateway/jwt", "patch": {"trusted_issuers_add": ["https://id.acme.io"]}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: applied; new-issuer tokens accepted; 401 rate back to baseline; old tokens still valid during overlap
Resolution: Root cause was the gateway trusting only the pre-migration issuer; adding the verified new issuer restored auth while preserving exact-issuer validation and the migration overlap.

Reasoning: Intermittent nonce mismatch right after fronting the login page with a CDN suggests the nonce the RP expected isn't the one in the id_token, likely a caching/session issue rather than crypto. Decode the id_token to grab its nonce.
<|call:decode_jwt|> {"token": "eyJ_id_token", "claims": ["nonce", "aud", "iat"]}
System Response: {"nonce": "n-7f3a", "aud": "web-rp", "iat": 1719490000}  <- signature valid and a nonce is present, so the token looks correct
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the id_token is valid and carries a nonce, so it looks fine; the mismatch must be that the RP expected a different nonce. Compare expected vs received for this session.
<|call:grep_app_logs|> {"service": "web-rp", "match": "nonce mismatch", "fields": ["expected_nonce", "received_nonce", "served_from"]}
System Response: expected_nonce=n-9b2c received_nonce=n-7f3a; the page that minted n-7f3a was served_from=cdn-cache
Recovery reasoning: The CDN cached the login HTML that embeds a per-request nonce, so multiple users share one stale nonce. Confirm the cache headers on the login route.
<|call:inspect_response_headers|> {"url": "https://app.acme.io/login", "fields": ["Cache-Control", "Set-Cookie"]}
System Response: Cache-Control: public, max-age=600  <- a login page that embeds a per-request nonce is being publicly cached
Recovery reasoning: A per-request nonce must never be cached. Mark the login route no-store so each login gets a fresh, session-bound nonce.
<|call:patch_validator_config|> {"target": "cdn/login-route", "patch": {"cache_control": "no-store, private", "vary": "Cookie"}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: login page no longer cached; each login mints a unique nonce; mismatch rate drops to 0
Resolution: Root cause was the CDN publicly caching a login page that embeds a per-request nonce so users shared a stale value; marking it no-store restored unique nonces and replay protection.

Reasoning: invalid_client on client_credentials right after a secret rotation means the secret the worker presents no longer matches what the IdP has. Replay the worker's token call to confirm it's an auth failure, not a network one.
<|call:replay_token_request|> {"grant_type": "client_credentials", "client_id": "sync-worker", "auth": "vault_current"}
System Response: 401 invalid_client {"error": "client authentication failed"}
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed it's client authentication, not connectivity. Check whether Vault actually rotated and which version the worker is reading.
<|call:get_client_config|> {"source": "vault", "path": "secret/sync-worker", "fields": ["version", "rotated_at", "current_secret"]}
System Response: version=7 rotated_at=00:00Z current_secret=cs_new; previous v6=cs_old  <- Vault rotated cleanly and serves the new secret, so Vault looks healthy
Recovery reasoning: Trap: Vault looks healthy and serves the new secret, but the IdP may never have received it. Compare against the IdP's active secret.
<|call:query_idp_logs|> {"event": "client_secret", "client": "sync-worker", "fields": ["active_secret_hash", "updated_at"]}
System Response: IdP active_secret_hash matches cs_old (v6); no update arrived at 00:00 -> rotation pushed to Vault only, never to the IdP
Recovery reasoning: The rotation updated Vault but never propagated to the IdP, so the worker presents a secret the IdP doesn't know. Push the new secret forward to the IdP rather than reverting to the retired one.
<|call:rotate_client_secret|> {"target": "idp/client/sync-worker", "patch": {"client_secret": "cs_new"}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: IdP now accepts cs_new (v7); client_credentials returns 200; sync jobs recover
Resolution: Root cause was a one-sided rotation (Vault updated, IdP not); propagating the new secret to the IdP restored auth without reusing the retired secret.

Reasoning: 429s from /token while IdP CPU sits at 35% means a rate limit is firing, not capacity. The question is which limit dimension. Check the rate-limit status the IdP reports for this client.
<|call:get_rate_limit_status|> {"endpoint": "/token", "client": "checkout-fleet"}
System Response: 200 {"limit": 6000, "remaining": 5800, "window": "60s"}  <- the per-client quota is barely touched and looks healthy/green
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the per-client limit is nearly untouched, so the throttle is coming from a different dimension. The fleet likely shares one egress IP, so check the limiter keyed by source IP.
<|call:get_rate_limit_status|> {"endpoint": "/token", "dimension": "source_ip"}
System Response: ip=203.0.113.7 limit=2000/60s remaining=0 throttled=true; 38 checkout pods all egress via this one NAT IP
Recovery reasoning: Every checkout pod shares one NAT egress IP that hits a per-IP limit while the generous per-client limit goes unused. Re-key the limiter on the authenticated client_id and trust the known egress IP.
<|call:patch_validator_config|> {"target": "idp/ratelimit/token", "patch": {"key": "client_id", "trusted_egress_add": ["203.0.113.7"]}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: limiter now keyed by client_id; 429s clear; checkout logins recover at full spike load
Resolution: Root cause was the /token limiter keyed by source IP while the whole fleet shared one NAT egress; re-keying by client_id removed the false throttle without disabling abuse protection.

Reasoning: state mismatch only on form_post (POST) callbacks right after enabling SameSite=Lax is the textbook symptom: Lax drops cookies on a cross-site POST, so the state cookie is missing at the callback. Inspect the callback request's cookies.
<|call:inspect_session_cookie|> {"flow": "callback", "method": "POST", "fields": ["oauth_state_cookie", "present"]}
System Response: the Set-Cookie on /authorize succeeded earlier, but at POST /callback oauth_state present=false  <- the cookie was set fine, so storage looks healthy
Recovery reasoning: Trap: the cookie was set successfully so storage looks healthy, but it isn't sent back on the cross-site POST. Confirm the cookie's SameSite attribute.
<|call:inspect_session_cookie|> {"cookie": "oauth_state", "fields": ["SameSite", "Secure", "Domain"]}
System Response: SameSite=Lax; Secure; Domain=app.acme.io -> Lax blocks sending it on a top-level cross-site POST from idp.acme.io
Recovery reasoning: Lax is exactly why the IdP's POST drops the cookie. The state cookie is a short-lived CSRF token that must cross sites by design, so SameSite=None;Secure is correct here. Scope the change to only the oauth_state cookie, not all session cookies.
<|call:patch_validator_config|> {"target": "spa-bff/cookies", "patch": {"oauth_state": {"SameSite": "None", "Secure": true, "Max-Age": 600}}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: oauth_state is now sent on the cross-site POST; state validates; mismatch rate drops to 0; other cookies stay Lax
Resolution: Root cause was SameSite=Lax blocking the state cookie on the IdP's cross-site form_post; setting only oauth_state to None;Secure restored the flow while keeping Lax on real session cookies.

Reasoning: 403 'actor not permitted' on a token-exchange delegation flow points at the act/may_act policy on the exchanged token, not the user's own authorization. Decode the exchanged token to inspect its actor claim.
<|call:decode_jwt|> {"token": "eyJ_exchanged", "claims": ["aud", "act", "scope", "sub"]}
System Response: {"aud": "ledger-svc", "sub": "user-123", "scope": "ledger.post", "act": {"sub": "api-gateway"}}  <- aud, scope, and actor all look correct
Recovery reasoning: Trap: aud, scope, and the actor are all present and look right, so the token seems fine; the gate must be ledger-svc's may_act allowlist, which the deploy likely changed. Check the token-exchange policy.
<|call:check_token_exchange_policy|> {"resource": "ledger-svc", "field": "permitted_actors"}
System Response: permitted_actors=["api-gateway-v2"]; the gateway still presents act.sub=api-gateway
Recovery reasoning: The policy expects api-gateway-v2 but the gateway sends api-gateway. Confirm the 16:30 deploy renamed it and dropped the old value rather than adding the new one.
<|call:query_idp_logs|> {"event": "policy_change", "since": "16:30Z", "resource": "ledger-svc", "field": "permitted_actors"}
System Response: change permitted_actors ["api-gateway"]->["api-gateway-v2"] by deploy ledger-policy@16:30 (rename dropped the old id)
Recovery reasoning: The old actor id was dropped instead of added alongside the new one. Re-add api-gateway so live tokens pass while keeping the may_act constraint intact.
<|call:check_token_exchange_policy|> {"resource": "ledger-svc", "patch": {"permitted_actors_add": ["api-gateway"]}, "dry_run": false}
System Response: api-gateway re-permitted as actor; exchanged tokens accepted; 403s clear
Resolution: Root cause was the policy deploy renaming the permitted actor and dropping the old id while the gateway still presents it; re-adding the actor restored delegated access without weakening the may_act constraint.
